(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["v-b"] = {"qs": [["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "An investor in the regular plan pays a slightly higher expense ratio than in the direct plan because:", ["The regular plan holds riskier securities", "The regular plan includes distribution commission", "The direct plan is subsidised by SEBI", "The direct plan does not pay any custodian fees"], 1, "Both plans hold the same portfolio. The regular plan's TER includes trail commission to the distributor, so its NAV grows slightly slower."], ["Scheme Related Information", "The riskometer of a scheme is meant to help investors:", ["Find out the exact return that the scheme will deliver over the next one year", "Compare tax rates on different schemes", "See the distributor's commission", "Understand the level of risk in the scheme at a glance"], 3, "The riskometer shows risk on a six-level scale from Low to Very High. It does not predict returns or show costs."], ["Taxation", "IDCW received by a resident investor from a mutual fund is taxed:", ["At a flat 5% for all investors", "Only if it exceeds ₹10 lakh a year", "Never, as it is exempt income", "At the investor's income tax slab rate"], 3, "IDCW is added to the investor's income and taxed at the applicable slab rate. The fund may also deduct TDS above a threshold."], ["Taxation", "Securities Transaction Tax on mutual funds is charged on:", ["Purchase of units of debt schemes", "Redemption of units of liquid funds", "Redemption of units of equity-oriented schemes", "IDCW paid by all schemes"], 2, "STT applies to the sale (redemption) of units of equity-oriented funds, at a very small rate collected by the fund. Debt fund transactions do not attract STT."], ["Scheme Related Information", "If a scheme's riskometer moves from 'High' to 'Very High', this means:", ["The assessed risk level of the scheme's portfolio has increased", "The scheme's returns are now guaranteed to rise in proportion to the higher level of risk", "The scheme will now be closed for redemptions", "The scheme's exit load has been removed"], 0, "Riskometers are reviewed monthly based on the portfolio. A move to Very High shows the portfolio has become riskier, which investors should note."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "A new cadre distributor's main source of income from AMCs is:", ["Trail commission based on the investor's assets", "A fixed salary paid by AMFI", "A share of the investor's capital gains", "A fee collected from SEBI"], 0, "Distributors earn trail commission, a percentage of the assets their clients hold in regular plans, paid periodically while the investment remains."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "The custodian of a mutual fund does NOT do which of the following?", ["Decide which stocks the scheme should buy", "Hold the scheme's securities safely", "Settle securities bought and sold", "Keep track of corporate actions on holdings"], 0, "The custodian keeps securities in safe custody, settles trades and tracks corporate actions like dividends and bonuses. Investment decisions are made by the AMC."], ["Performance of Mutual Funds", "Which comparison is appropriate when evaluating a large cap fund?", ["Comparing it with large cap peers and index", "Comparing it with a liquid fund's return", "Comparing it with a bank savings rate only", "Comparing it with a gold ETF's return"], 0, "Fair comparison needs similar risk and objectives. A large cap fund should be compared with its category peers and a large cap benchmark."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Which of these is an investor's right in a mutual fund scheme?", ["Timely payment of redemption proceeds", "Receiving a guaranteed minimum return", "Choosing the stocks the fund buys", "Getting back losses from the AMC"], 0, "Investors have rights to information, timely payment and fair treatment. Returns are not guaranteed and investment decisions remain with the AMC."], ["Performance of Mutual Funds", "A scheme's NAV rose from ₹40 to ₹46 in one year with no IDCW. What is the return?", ["6%", "13%", "46%", "15%"], 3, "Return = (46 − 40) / 40 × 100 = 15%. ₹6 is the gain in rupees, not the percentage, and 13% wrongly divides by the ending NAV."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Where would an investor find the exit load applicable to a particular scheme?", ["In the AMC's annual report only", "In the Scheme Information Document and KIM", "In the investor's PAN card details", "In the stock exchange's daily bulletin"], 1, "Loads are terms of the offer, so they are disclosed in the SID and summarised in the KIM. Factsheets also usually show the current exit load."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which asset class gives a part-owner's share in a company's profits and growth?", ["Fixed deposits", "Government bonds", "Savings bank accounts", "Equity"], 3, "Equity shares represent ownership, so shareholders share in profits and growth (and losses). Deposits and bonds are loans to the issuer that pay interest instead."], ["Investment Landscape", "'Do not put all your eggs in one basket' describes the principle of:", ["Leverage", "Speculation", "Diversification", "Market timing"], 2, "Diversification means spreading money across many investments so that a problem in one does not hurt the whole portfolio badly. It reduces risk specific to any single investment."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "A debt fund primarily invests in:", ["Bonds, government securities and money market instruments", "Shares of listed companies across large, mid and small caps", "Real estate properties", "Physical gold and silver"], 0, "Debt funds lend money by buying fixed income securities and earn interest and price gains. Equity funds buy shares, and gold ETFs hold gold."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "The mutual fund trust is registered with which regulator before it can launch schemes?", ["RBI", "IRDAI", "PFRDA", "SEBI"], 3, "Every mutual fund in India must be registered with SEBI under the SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996. RBI regulates banks, IRDAI insurance and PFRDA pensions."], ["Taxation", "A scheme qualifies as 'equity-oriented' for tax purposes if it invests at least what share of its assets in domestic equity shares?", ["65%", "50%", "80%", "35%"], 0, "An equity-oriented fund must invest at least 65% in equity shares of domestic listed companies. Such schemes get the concessional tax treatment applicable to equity."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "A distributor's EUIN is unique to:", ["The scheme in which the investor invests", "The investor's bank account", "The individual employee who serves the investor", "The stock exchange used for transactions"], 2, "EUIN identifies the specific employee or relationship manager of a distributor who served the investor. It helps track advice quality and prevent mis-selling."], ["Mutual Fund Products for New Cadre Distributors", "An index fund's NAV moves slightly differently from its index mainly because of:", ["The fund manager's stock-picking bets", "Expenses and cash holdings of the fund", "A guaranteed bonus paid by the AMC", "SEBI's daily adjustments to NAV"], 1, "Index funds copy the index, but expenses, cash for redemptions and timing of trades cause small gaps known as tracking error. They do not involve active stock picking."], ["Performance of Mutual Funds", "A scheme's benchmark is used to:", ["Decide the investor's tax rate", "Judge if the manager added value", "Fix the exit load of the scheme", "Calculate the distributor's commission"], 1, "A benchmark represents the market the scheme invests in. Beating it after costs shows the manager added value; lagging it shows the opposite."], ["Mutual Fund Products for New Cadre Distributors", "An ETF's price on the stock exchange during the day is determined by:", ["Buying and selling by investors on the exchange, close to its intraday value", "Only the NAV declared by the AMC at the end of the previous business day", "A fixed price set by SEBI for each year", "The AMC's decision each morning"], 0, "ETF units trade on exchanges like shares, so their market price moves through the day with demand and supply. Market makers help keep it close to the indicative NAV."], ["Mutual Fund Products for New Cadre Distributors", "Which of these is TRUE of a large cap fund?", ["It invests only in companies ranked 251st and below by full market capitalisation", "It must hold at least 50% in bonds", "It guarantees returns higher than bank deposits", "It invests mainly in the 100 largest companies by market capitalisation"], 3, "Large cap funds invest at least 80% in the top 100 companies by full market capitalisation, which are generally more established and less volatile than mid or small caps."], ["Investment Landscape", "The main difference between saving and investing is that investing:", ["Keeps all the money only as cash at home or in a locker, so that it is always within reach", "Puts money into assets aiming for growth, accepting some risk", "Never involves any risk to capital", "Is meant only for very wealthy people"], 1, "Saving sets aside money from income; investing deploys it into assets such as equity, debt or gold to earn returns, usually with some risk. Anyone can invest small amounts through SIPs."], ["Investment Landscape", "Which of these generally offers the highest long-term return potential along with the highest short-term volatility?", ["Bank fixed deposits of five years", "Treasury bills", "Equity shares", "Savings bank deposits"], 2, "Equity has historically offered the highest long-term growth but its prices swing sharply in the short run. Deposits and T-bills are more stable but offer lower returns."], ["Taxation", "An investor sells equity fund units after 18 months at a gain. The gain is treated as:", ["Short-term capital gain", "Income from salary", "Long-term capital gain", "Business income"], 2, "For equity-oriented funds, units held for more than 12 months are long-term capital assets. Eighteen months exceeds this, so the gain is long-term."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "The statutory auditor of a mutual fund scheme is responsible for:", ["Selecting the stocks that the scheme buys and deciding when to sell them", "Processing investor KYC applications", "Auditing the scheme's accounts and reporting on them", "Holding the scheme's securities"], 2, "The auditor examines and reports on the scheme's accounts independently. Stock selection belongs to the AMC, KYC to KRAs and intermediaries, and custody to the custodian."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Which entity is responsible for keeping a check on the AMC to protect unitholders?", ["The trustees", "The distributors", "The stock exchanges", "The rating agencies"], 0, "Trustees supervise the AMC and ensure it acts in unitholders' interests and complies with regulations. Distributors sell schemes; exchanges and rating agencies have different roles."], ["Investment Landscape", "Money kept idle in a locker for 10 years while prices rise by about 6% a year will:", ["Keep exactly the same purchasing power", "Lose purchasing power over time", "Grow in value at the rate of inflation", "Earn interest from the Reserve Bank"], 1, "Cash earns nothing, so rising prices reduce what the same amount can buy. This is why savings need to be invested in assets that aim to beat inflation."], ["Investment Landscape", "An investor needs money for a child's school fees in six months. Which feature matters most when choosing where to keep this money?", ["Highest possible long-term growth", "Tax benefit under a long lock-in", "Exposure to small companies", "Safety and liquidity"], 3, "Short-term goals need money that will be available and not fall in value. Seeking high growth with volatile assets could leave a shortfall exactly when the fees are due."], ["Scheme Related Information", "A monthly factsheet issued by an AMC typically shows:", ["Holdings, AUM, TER and performance", "The personal details of each investor", "The names of investors who redeemed", "Guaranteed returns for the next year"], 0, "Factsheets summarise key scheme data such as top holdings, sector mix, AUM, TER, fund manager and performance versus benchmark, helping investors monitor their schemes."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "When a bank sells mutual fund schemes to its customers, it acts as a:", ["Trustee of the mutual fund", "Custodian of the investor's units", "Regulator of the AMC", "Mutual fund distributor holding an ARN"], 3, "Banks distribute mutual funds as corporate distributors with an ARN, and their staff who sell must be NISM-certified with an EUIN."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Assets Under Management (AUM) of a scheme refers to:", ["The total market value of investments managed in the scheme", "The minimum investment amount that a new investor must put into the scheme", "The AMC's own share capital", "The profit earned by the fund manager"], 0, "AUM is the total value of money the scheme manages on behalf of investors. It changes daily with market movements, purchases and redemptions."], ["Mutual Fund Products for New Cadre Distributors", "Which product would suit an investor who wants both a tax deduction under the old regime and long-term equity growth?", ["An overnight fund", "A liquid fund", "A gold ETF", "An ELSS scheme"], 3, "ELSS is an equity scheme with a three-year lock-in that qualifies for a tax deduction under the old tax regime. Overnight, liquid and gold ETFs do not offer this deduction."], ["Performance of Mutual Funds", "A fund that returned 40% last year but lost heavily in other years may be less attractive than one with steady returns because:", ["High returns are not allowed under SEBI rules", "Steady funds are guaranteed by the AMC", "A 40% return is always taxed at a higher rate", "Consistency is a better guide than one year"], 3, "One outstanding year can be luck or due to a short-lived theme. Consistent performance across years and market conditions is a more reliable signal of quality."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Which constituent of a mutual fund makes buy and sell decisions for the scheme's portfolio?", ["The custodian of the fund", "The AMC, through its fund managers", "The registrar and transfer agent", "The investors, by voting each week"], 1, "The AMC employs fund managers and research analysts who decide which securities to buy and sell in line with the scheme's objective. Investors do not vote on daily decisions."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Before recommending a scheme, a distributor should first:", ["Recommend the scheme that paid the highest return last month", "Recommend whatever the AMC is promoting this quarter", "Ask the investor to sign a blank form", "Understand the investor's goals, horizon and risk appetite"], 3, "Suitable advice starts with knowing the investor. Recommending based on recent returns or AMC campaigns, or taking blank signed forms, can lead to mis-selling."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Mutual funds in India are mainly regulated under:", ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996", "The Banking Regulation Act, 1949", "The Insurance Act, 1938", "The Payment and Settlement Systems Act, 2007"], 0, "SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 1996 set rules for sponsors, trustees, AMCs, schemes, investments and disclosures. Banking and insurance laws apply to other sectors."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "A dispute with a mutual fund remains unresolved even after SCORES. The investor can next use:", ["The Consumer Court only, with no other route", "A complaint to AMFI's distributor desk", "The securities market Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) platform", "A complaint to the stock exchange where units are not listed"], 2, "SEBI's ODR mechanism allows investors to resolve disputes with market intermediaries, including mutual funds, through online conciliation and arbitration."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "If an AMC proposes to merge a scheme with another scheme, changing its fundamental attributes, existing investors must be:", ["Forced to remain invested in the merged scheme", "Charged a higher exit load to discourage exit", "Paid a guaranteed bonus by the AMC", "Given an option to exit without exit load"], 3, "A merger that changes fundamental attributes requires informing unitholders and offering them an exit at NAV without load during the specified period."], ["Performance of Mutual Funds", "Two equity funds hold the same portfolio. Fund A has a TER of 0.5% and Fund B 1.5%. Over 20 years, Fund A will most likely:", ["Deliver higher returns because of lower costs", "Deliver lower returns because cheap funds perform worse", "Deliver exactly the same returns as Fund B", "Be closed by SEBI for charging too little"], 0, "With identical portfolios, the only difference is cost. A 1% lower expense each year compounds into a meaningful difference over 20 years."], ["Investment Landscape", "Gold is often added to a portfolio mainly because it:", ["Tends to behave differently from equities and can hedge against uncertainty", "Pays a fixed rate of interest every year, like a government savings bond held to maturity", "Has no price risk at all", "Is fully guaranteed by the government"], 0, "Gold often holds value during market stress or currency weakness, so it can diversify a portfolio. It pays no interest and its price can be volatile."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "AMFI's code of conduct for distributors requires them to:", ["Guarantee returns on equity schemes", "Recommend suitable schemes and disclose key facts", "Promise to buy back units at a higher price", "Share the investor's details with other agents"], 1, "The code requires distributors to act in investors' interest, give suitable advice, provide scheme documents and keep information confidential."], ["Mutual Fund Products for New Cadre Distributors", "To buy units of an ETF on the exchange, an investor generally needs:", ["Only a bank fixed deposit account", "A loan from the AMC", "A demat and trading account", "A Public Provident Fund account"], 2, "ETFs are listed securities, so buying them requires a trading account with a broker and a demat account to hold the units, just like shares."], ["Taxation", "Who deducts TDS on IDCW paid to a resident investor, where applicable?", ["The mutual fund, before paying the IDCW", "The investor's employer", "The investor's bank, at the time of credit", "The distributor, from its commission"], 0, "The mutual fund deducts TDS on IDCW above the prescribed threshold before paying it out, and the investor can claim credit for it while filing the return."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "If an AMC does not resolve an investor's complaint satisfactorily, the investor can escalate it online through:", ["The RBI Integrated Ombudsman", "The Insurance Ombudsman", "SEBI's SCORES platform", "The local police station"], 2, "SCORES is SEBI's online grievance system for complaints against listed companies and intermediaries, including mutual funds. Unresolved disputes can then go to online dispute resolution."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "In the mutual fund structure, the sponsor is comparable to:", ["A customer of a bank", "An independent auditor", "A stock exchange", "The promoter of a company"], 3, "Just as a promoter sets up a company, the sponsor establishes the mutual fund, sets up the trust and the AMC, and must meet SEBI eligibility conditions."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Which of the following is TRUE about returns from a mutual fund?", ["They are guaranteed by the AMC, subject to the scheme completing three years", "They depend on the performance of the underlying investments", "They are fixed by SEBI every year", "They are insured by DICGC"], 1, "Mutual fund returns are market-linked and are not guaranteed by anyone. DICGC insurance applies to bank deposits, not mutual fund units."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "Banks that receive investors' application money on behalf of a scheme are called:", ["Custodian banks of the scheme", "Sponsor banks", "Collecting bankers", "Trustee banks"], 2, "Collecting bankers accept investment money for the scheme at their branches or through electronic channels. A custodian holds the securities, a different role."], ["Scheme Related Information", "The investment objective stated in an SID tells the investor:", ["The exact return the scheme will earn", "The tax rate on redemption proceeds", "What the scheme aims to achieve and broadly how", "The NAV on the date of allotment"], 2, "The objective describes the scheme's goal, such as long-term capital growth by investing in large cap equity. It does not promise any return."], ["Mutual Fund Products for New Cadre Distributors", "An investor starts an ELSS SIP of ₹2,000 a month. When can the instalment invested in March 2026 be redeemed at the earliest?", ["March 2027", "March 2029", "March 2031", "Any time after March 2026"], 1, "Each ELSS instalment has its own three-year lock-in from the date of investment. The March 2026 instalment can therefore be redeemed only from March 2029."], ["Performance of Mutual Funds", "An investor bought a unit at ₹20, received an IDCW of ₹1, and the unit is now worth ₹21. What is the total return for the year?", ["5%", "10%", "15%", "21%"], 1, "Total return = (21 − 20 + 1) / 20 × 100 = 10%. Ignoring the IDCW would give only 5%, which understates what the investor earned."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Periodic disclosure of the scheme's portfolio is useful because it lets investors:", ["Avoid paying any expense ratio", "Check whether the fund follows its stated mandate", "Trade the underlying shares directly", "Change the scheme's investment objective"], 1, "By seeing actual holdings, investors can confirm that the scheme invests as promised and judge concentration and credit quality."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Which regulator's rules govern how mutual funds may advertise their schemes?", ["AMFI alone, with no SEBI role", "RBI", "Ministry of Finance only", "SEBI"], 3, "SEBI's mutual fund regulations include an advertisement code that sets rules on disclosures, standard warnings and performance presentation. AMFI issues supporting industry guidelines."], ["Taxation", "An investor switches from an equity fund to a debt fund of the same AMC. For tax purposes, the switch is:", ["A tax-free transfer within the same AMC", "Treated as a redemption, so capital gains tax may apply", "Taxed only when the debt fund is sold", "Treated as a fresh gift to the AMC"], 1, "A switch is a sale of units in one scheme and a purchase in another. Any gain on the units switched out is taxable, and exit load may also apply."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "A distributor collects cash of ₹1 lakh from a client and promises to deposit it into a scheme later. This practice is:", ["Acceptable if the distributor gives a handwritten receipt", "Not appropriate; money must go directly to the scheme", "Encouraged to save the investor's time", "Mandatory for investors without bank accounts"], 1, "Investor money must go directly to the scheme through permitted payment modes, not through the distributor. Collecting money personally is risky and against the code of conduct."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "Which of these is an example of a passive fund?", ["A flexi cap fund selecting stocks freely", "An index fund tracking the Nifty 50", "A value fund picking cheap stocks", "A focused fund holding 25 stocks"], 1, "A passive fund simply replicates an index without stock selection by the manager. Flexi cap, value and focused funds are actively managed."], ["Scheme Related Information", "An investor wants information about the mutual fund's sponsor, trustees and AMC directors. The most relevant document is the:", ["Key Information Memorandum", "Account statement", "Riskometer", "Statement of Additional Information"], 3, "The SAI contains statutory information common to all schemes of the fund house, such as details of the sponsor, AMC, trustees and their directors."], ["Mutual Fund Products for New Cadre Distributors", "A sectoral fund investing only in banking shares is riskier than a diversified equity fund because:", ["It is not regulated by SEBI", "It cannot be redeemed for five years", "Its performance depends heavily on one sector", "It invests only in government bonds"], 2, "Concentration in one sector means a downturn in that sector hits the whole portfolio. A diversified fund spreads investments across many sectors."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "A 'unit' of a mutual fund scheme represents:", ["A fixed deposit placed with the AMC that earns a stated rate of interest", "A loan given by the investor to SEBI", "A proportionate share in the scheme's assets", "A share in the AMC company's profits"], 2, "Each unit is a proportional claim on the scheme's net assets. Unitholders do not own shares of the AMC and do not lend money to the fund."], ["Taxation", "Investment in ELSS is eligible for a tax deduction only for taxpayers who:", ["Opt for the new tax regime", "Are non-residents", "Earn only capital gains", "Opt for the old tax regime"], 3, "The new tax regime does not allow most deductions, including the ELSS deduction. Taxpayers must choose the old regime to claim this benefit."], ["Investor Services", "An investor wants to change the bank account registered in a folio. The request should be made:", ["With a written request and proof of the new bank account", "By telling the distributor on a phone call only", "By writing a new bank number on a redemption form", "By sending an SMS to SEBI"], 0, "Change of bank mandate requires a signed request with proof such as a cancelled cheque or bank statement. This protects investors from fraud."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "An investor can transact in mutual funds without any distributor by:", ["Calling SEBI's helpline", "Buying the direct plan from the AMC", "Writing to the stock exchange", "Asking the custodian bank"], 1, "Direct plans can be bought from the AMC's own website, office or app, or through RTA and other direct channels, without a distributor and without commission."], ["Performance of Mutual Funds", "Which statement about past performance is correct?", ["It guarantees at least the same return next year", "It is the only factor needed to choose a fund", "It does not guarantee future returns", "It is fixed by SEBI for every category"], 2, "Markets change and so do fund returns. Past performance is useful for judging consistency, but it is not a promise of future results, as every scheme document states."], ["Investor Services", "Redemption proceeds of most equity schemes are normally paid to the investor within:", ["15 working days", "30 days", "3 working days", "6 months"], 2, "SEBI requires most schemes to pay redemption proceeds within three working days of the request (with some exceptions for schemes investing overseas or in special situations)."], ["Investor Services", "An investor with a lump sum in a liquid fund wants to move ₹10,000 every month into an equity fund. The suitable facility is a:", ["Systematic Transfer Plan (STP)", "Systematic Investment Plan from the bank", "Systematic Withdrawal Plan to the bank", "Fresh NFO application each month"], 0, "An STP transfers a fixed amount periodically from one scheme to another of the same fund house, letting the lump sum enter equity gradually."], ["Legal Structure of Mutual Funds", "AMFI is best described as:", ["A government department that regulates AMCs and issues their licences", "The industry association of SEBI-registered AMCs", "A bank that holds mutual fund money", "A company that manages mutual fund schemes"], 1, "AMFI is an industry body formed by AMCs. It is not a statutory regulator; it registers distributors (ARN), frames codes of conduct and works on investor education."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "The 'folio' in a mutual fund helps an investor to:", ["Get a guaranteed return from the AMC on all the schemes held together in that account", "Avoid paying any tax on gains", "Hold all investments in schemes of one fund house under one account number", "Trade units on the stock exchange"], 2, "A folio number is like an account number with a fund house. All investments in its schemes can be grouped in one folio, which simplifies statements and servicing."], ["Taxation", "A long-term capital loss on mutual fund units can be set off against:", ["Any salary income", "Only long-term capital gains", "Interest from fixed deposits", "Short-term gains and salary"], 1, "Long-term capital losses can only be set off against long-term capital gains, in the same year or the next eight years. They cannot reduce salary or interest income."], ["Performance of Mutual Funds", "Investment of ₹1,00,000 grew to ₹1,50,000 in 3 years. The absolute return is:", ["16.7%", "14.5%", "50%", "150%"], 2, "Absolute return = (1,50,000 − 1,00,000) / 1,00,000 = 50%. 16.7% is a simple yearly average, and 14.5% is the compounded annual rate, both different measures."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Which detail must a distributor's application form submission carry so that commission is paid correctly?", ["The investor's mobile network provider", "The fund manager's name", "The distributor's ARN code", "The investor's employer's PAN"], 2, "The ARN code on the form links the transaction to the distributor, so the AMC knows whom to pay trail commission. Without it the investment goes into the direct plan."], ["Investor Services", "For an equity scheme, a purchase of ₹50,000 received by 3 pm on a business day, with funds credited before the cut-off, gets:", ["The NAV of the previous day", "The NAV of the same day", "The NAV fixed at the NFO", "The NAV of the next month"], 1, "For equity schemes, the applicable NAV depends on when funds are realised in the scheme's account. If the money is available before the 3 pm cut-off, the same day's NAV applies."], ["Investor Services", "Under the growth option of a scheme, the investor's gains are:", ["Paid out as cash every month", "Converted to fixed deposits", "Retained in the scheme and reflected in a rising NAV", "Distributed among other unitholders"], 2, "In the growth option, income and gains stay invested, so the NAV increases. Gains are realised only when units are redeemed."], ["Investor Services", "A KYC done through a KYC Registration Agency (KRA) allows an investor to:", ["Skip PAN for all future investments", "Invest across intermediaries without repeating KYC", "Avoid paying any tax on mutual fund gains", "Invest only in the schemes of one AMC"], 1, "KYC recorded with a KRA is shared across SEBI-registered intermediaries, so investors do not have to repeat the full process for each AMC or broker."], ["Scheme Related Information", "Which document must be given along with every mutual fund application form?", ["Statement of Additional Information", "Annual report of the AMC", "Key Information Memorandum", "Trust deed of the mutual fund"], 2, "The KIM, a short summary of the SID, must accompany every application form so that investors see the key features before investing."], ["Fund Distribution and Channel Management", "Which of these is an unethical practice for a distributor?", ["Encouraging frequent switching to earn more commission", "Explaining the risks of the scheme clearly", "Giving the investor the KIM with the form", "Keeping records of the investor's risk profile"], 0, "Churning investors between schemes for higher commission harms investors and violates the AMFI code of conduct. The other practices are good conduct."], ["Investment Landscape", "If ₹50,000 is invested at 8% a year compounded annually, what is it worth after 2 years?", ["₹58,320", "₹58,000", "₹54,000", "₹59,000"], 0, "Value = 50,000 × 1.08 × 1.08 = ₹58,320. ₹58,000 is simple interest of ₹4,000 a year, which ignores interest earned on interest."], ["Investor Services", "Why should an investor register a nominee in a mutual fund folio?", ["To get a higher NAV on redemption", "To avoid paying exit load", "To allow the nominee to trade on the investor's behalf now", "To simplify transmission after the investor's death"], 3, "A nominee can claim the units easily after the holder's death, avoiding lengthy legal processes. A nominee has no rights while the investor is alive."], ["Mutual Fund Products for New Cadre Distributors", "A Gold ETF is suitable for an investor who wants:", ["Exposure to gold prices without holding physical gold", "A fixed interest income credited to the bank account on the same date every month", "Ownership of gold mining company shares only", "A guaranteed return linked to the repo rate"], 0, "A Gold ETF holds physical gold of defined purity and tracks domestic gold prices. It avoids storage and purity worries but pays no interest."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "How can a small investor with ₹500 a month gain exposure to many companies at once?", ["By buying one share of every Nifty company", "By opening multiple savings accounts", "By buying a single small cap share", "By investing through a mutual fund SIP"], 3, "Mutual funds pool money from many investors, so even ₹500 buys a slice of a diversified portfolio. Buying one share of every index company would cost far more."], ["Investor Services", "An investor wants to invest ₹5,000 every month automatically into an equity scheme. The suitable facility is a:", ["Systematic Withdrawal Plan (SWP)", "One-time lumpsum purchase", "Switch from another scheme", "Systematic Investment Plan (SIP)"], 3, "A SIP debits a fixed amount from the bank account at regular intervals and buys units, building discipline and averaging purchase cost over time."], ["Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund", "A hybrid fund is one that invests in:", ["Only overnight money market instruments with a maturity of one business day", "Only shares of one sector", "Only units of foreign mutual funds", "A combination of asset classes such as equity and debt"], 3, "Hybrid funds mix asset classes, usually equity and debt (sometimes gold), in proportions defined by their SEBI category, aiming to balance growth and stability."]]};
